        "task": "Audit admin role-check coverage in /workspace/server (call get_threat_model target /workspace/server first).\n\nSHELL QUIRK: exec_command default pipes swallow output — always pass tty:true.\n\nKNOWN: src/routes/admin.routes.ts mounts under /api/admin with router.use(requireAuth) + router.use(requireAdmin) — appears solid, but audit INSIDE for routes that opt out or re-mount. src/routes/pharmacy.routes.ts mounts at /api/admin/pharmacies (app.ts line ~363) — UNVERIFIED whether it has its own admin guard. Also many privileged actions live OUTSIDE admin.routes.ts: settings mutations, push campaigns, credits, tickets management — find where they live and whether OWNER role can reach them.\n\nDO:\n1. Read pharmacy.routes.ts fully — does it apply requireAuth/requireAdmin? Any public routes under /api/admin/*?\n2. Enumerate ALL mounts in app.ts and classify each router: public / requireAuth only / requireAuth+requireAdmin / requireDoctor. Check doctor.routes.ts, doctor-share.routes.ts, doctor-question.routes.ts guards (doctor JWT type guard?), consulta.ts (public CRM consultation?), ticket.routes.ts (admin manage actions?).\n3. Check for role escalation paths: can an OWNER set role='ADMIN' via profile update / mass assignment (auth.routes.ts register/update, patient create, billing, data export/import)? Search schema.prisma for User.role default and any route that writes user.role.\n4. Check utils/settings.ts getSettings/saveSettings callers — who can mutate AppSettings (MOTD, premium perks, AI config)?\n5. Check audit-log/accessAudit middleware for bypasses (paths that skip audit).\n6. Static only. For each surface: record_coverage (surface e.g. 'pharmacy.routes.ts', risk 'BFLA/admin authorization') with honest outcome; note 'admin-guard-audit' (findings) with candidates file:line.\n7. Do NOT file reports — validation agents follow up.",
